{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1852178230/13/codegen_prompts/5.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1852178230/13`\n- 题号：`5`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1852178230/13/5.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"5\">\n<description>\n根据种子对象的同心嵌套层数确定放大倍数，将最内层核心按该倍数放大后，用各层颜色从内到外重新包裹，在指定位置绘制放大版同心结构。\n\nInput structure\n- 背景为 black。网格中有若干独立的小型\"种子对象\"，彼此至少间隔 2 像素。\n- 每个种子对象是一个正方形的同心嵌套结构：最外层是一圈颜色 A 的 1 像素宽正方形框，下一层是一圈颜色 B 的 1 像素宽正方形框……直到最内层是一个 1x1 的实心核心像素。每层颜色各不相同，层数在 2-4 层之间（含核心）。例如，一个 3 层种子对象外观为 5x5 正方形：最外圈为 red、中间圈为 blue、中心 1x1 为 yellow。\n- 网格中另有若干 1x1 的 grey 标记像素，数量与种子对象相同，散布在网格的其他位置。\n\nRule\n- 将种子对象按其左上角坐标从上到下、从左到右排序；将 grey 标记点按相同方式排序；按顺序一一配对。\n- 对每个种子对象，统计其嵌套层数 n（包含核心层）。\n- 放大倍数等于 n：取种子最内层核心的 1x1 像素，放大为 n x n 的实心色块。\n- 从放大后的核心开始，向外逐层包裹 1 像素宽的正方形边框，颜色顺序与种子对象从内到外的层序一致（即倒数第二层颜色紧贴放大核心外侧，最外层颜色在最外面）。\n- 将最终构建的放大同心结构以其对应 grey 标记点为中心绘制到网格上，覆盖该标记点。原始种子对象保留在原位不变。\n\nKey insight\n- 解题者需要两步非平凡推理：(1) 识别种子对象的同心嵌套层结构并数出层数，理解层数本身决定了核心的放大倍数（而非某个固定常数）；(2) 放大操作仅针对最内层核心，各层颜色按照原始从内到外的顺序重新构建——放大结果不是种子的整体等比缩放，而是核心的选择性放大加上颜色层序的忠实重现。\n</description>\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/2ba387bc, eval/a395ee82, train/17829a00, eval/8698868d, eval/581f7754, train/8a6d367c, eval/3e6067c3, train/14b8e18c\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2ba387bc\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3822\nmechanism=0.5000\nlexical=0.0111\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:frame, object:sprite, object:square, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/2ba387bc.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/2ba387bc.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort scattered 4×4 squares into two columns by type: frames left, solids right.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Multiple 4×4 objects on bg.\n- Frames: colored 1px border, bg-filled 2×2 interior.\n- Solids: entirely filled with one non-bg color.\n\nRule:\n- Classify each 4×4 object as frame or solid by its interior.\n- Sort each group top-to-bottom by input row.\n- Stack frames in left column (cols 0–3), solids in right (cols 4–7).\n- Output is 8 wide; height = 4 × max(#frames, #solids), bg-padded.\n\nKey insight: Frame vs solid is determined solely by whether the 2×2 interior matches bg.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"hollow square\",\n  \"dst\": \"into left slot\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.pos.row.order == d.pos.row.order\",\n  \"src2\": \"solid square\",\n  \"dst2\": \"into right slot\",\n  \"match_cond2\": \"s.pos.row.order == d.pos.row.order\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"move squares from top to bottom, then from left to right\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a395ee82\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2955\nmechanism=0.3254\nlexical=0.0110\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:count, relation:overlap\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/a395ee82.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/a395ee82.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nStamp translated copies of a monochrome template guided by a singleton pixel layout.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A large monochrome shape (template, color T).\n- Singleton pixels elsewhere: exactly one is color T (layout origin); the rest share color O (overlap color).\n- Singletons sit on a uniform-spacing lattice.\n\nRule:\n- Convert each singleton's offset from the origin into coarse lattice units.\n- Place a template copy at each offset, scaled by template height/width.\n- The origin's copy is painted O instead of T.\n- Any pixel covered by two or more copies is also recolored to O.\n\nKey insight: The single T-colored singleton identifies both the origin position and the \"self\" copy, which is uniquely recolored.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"marker pixel set s with different colored center pixel and peripheral pixels\",\n  \"dst\": \"monochrome sprite d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.center.color == d.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"s.peripheral_pixels -> d. d.color -> s.peripheral_pixels.color\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"17829a00\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3197\nmechanism=0.4082\nlexical=0.0107\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:frame, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:overlap\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/17829a00.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/17829a00.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove each colored object vertically until it touches the matching top or bottom border bar.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The top row is a full-width bar in one foreground color and the bottom row is a full-width bar in another.\n- All other foreground pixels are 8-connected monochrome objects whose color matches one of those bars.\n\nRule:\n- Top-bar-colored objects move upward so their bbox top is row 1.\n- Bottom-bar-colored objects move downward so their bbox bottom is the row above the bottom bar.\n- A downward-moving one-column segment whose landing overlaps its source becomes the continuous vertical segment swept between them.\n\nKey insight:\nThe horizontal bars are destination anchors: color determines which border an object slides toward, while columns and object shape are otherwise preserved.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"non-border 8-connected foreground objects matching a full-width border bar color\",\n  \"path\": \"vertical path toward the same-color top or bottom border bar\",\n  \"dst\": \"touch the same-color border bar\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"translate each object to the adjacent row of that bar; a downward one-column segment whose landing overlaps its source fills the continuous swept segment\",\n  \"transform\": \"redraw the border bars and moved objects on the bg\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8698868d\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3211\nmechanism=0.3808\nlexical=0.0108\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:frame, object:sprite, object:square, operation:count, operation:sort_order\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/8698868d.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/8698868d.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMatch sprites to frames by connected-hole count, then nest each sprite inside its frame.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Large hollow squares (frames) arranged in a grid; smaller filled squares (sprites) placed elsewhere.\n- Each frame and sprite has a distinct color. Sprites may contain holes (non-sprite-color pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Frames form a regular grid; some may be bg-colored (invisible).\n- Count connected hole regions inside each frame's interior and each sprite.\n- Pair each frame with the sprite sharing the same hole count.\n- Place the matched sprite inside the frame border; recolor sprite holes to the frame's color.\n\nKey insight: The matching key is the number of connected hole components, not hole area or color.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"sprites (small filled squares with holes) outside the frame grid\",\n  \"dst\": \"place into frames d (large hollow squares) forming a grid\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"count of connected hole regions (using scipy.ndimage.label)\",\n  \"transform\": \"sprite holes become frame color when placed inside\",\n  \"notes\": \"frames may include background-colored (invisible) frames; sprite fill ratio can be low due to holes\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"581f7754\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2992\nmechanism=0.3484\nlexical=0.0112\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:frame, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:sort_order, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/581f7754.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/581f7754.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSlide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color \"marker\" pixel.\n- Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n- Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
